1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,360
Open A12, and give this the same twenty minutes every workshop in this course has

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:05,979
asked for.

3
00:00:05,979 --> 00:00:07,463
The bar is the same too.

4
00:00:07,463 --> 00:00:08,205
Not perfect.

5
00:00:08,205 --> 00:00:13,215
Complete, with a real number in every branch, even where that number is an honest

6
00:00:13,215 --> 00:00:16,803
"not yet measured" rather than a guess dressed up as data.

7
00:00:16,803 --> 00:00:19,586
This workshop doesn't introduce anything new.

8
00:00:19,586 --> 00:00:24,410
Every block on the next slide is a direct check against something this section

9
00:00:24,410 --> 00:00:29,420
already taught you, which means the twenty minutes should feel like assembly, not

10
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:30,039
discovery.

11
00:00:30,039 --> 00:00:35,173
If it feels like discovery somewhere, that block is this workshop's actual finding.

12
00:00:35,173 --> 00:00:38,327
Treat the twenty minutes as a floor, not a ceiling.

13
00:00:38,327 --> 00:00:43,213
A tree assembled quickly from already-solid numbers is a genuine success, not a

14
00:00:43,213 --> 00:00:45,069
shortcut to feel guilty about.

15
00:00:45,069 --> 00:00:50,141
The workshop rewards a team that did the earlier work honestly by making this part

16
00:00:50,141 --> 00:00:50,450
fast.

17
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,717
Notice the first two blocks are a direct check, not new work.

18
00:00:54,717 --> 00:00:59,836
If 9.1's three metrics and 9.2's request_id thread are already solid, this is a fast

19
00:00:59,836 --> 00:01:02,457
eight minutes confirming it, not a rebuild.

20
00:01:02,457 --> 00:01:07,576
Most of this workshop's real time goes to whichever block turns out to be the honest

21
00:01:07,576 --> 00:01:07,819
gap.

22
00:01:07,819 --> 00:01:12,755
Minutes eight through twelve ask you to actually open your last A/B test's setup,

23
00:01:12,755 --> 00:01:14,340
not recall it from memory.

24
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:18,849
Randomised by user, version pinned for the duration, sized larger than the

25
00:01:18,849 --> 00:01:20,129
calculator suggested.

26
00:01:20,129 --> 00:01:24,821
If you've never run one, that's a legitimate answer for this block too, noted

27
00:01:24,821 --> 00:01:26,650
honestly rather than invented.

28
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:31,647
The feedback loop block, minutes twelve through sixteen, is the one most workshops

29
00:01:31,647 --> 00:01:36,400
before this one have found the hardest, because it demands a real example: one

30
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:41,336
rejected case, actually traced to a fix or a golden-set row, not a description of

31
00:01:41,336 --> 00:01:43,042
the process in the abstract.

32
00:01:43,042 --> 00:01:48,161
The last block, the leadership dashboard, only works once the first four are honest,

33
00:01:48,161 --> 00:01:51,452
which is why it sits at the end rather than the start.

34
00:01:51,452 --> 00:01:56,571
A dashboard assembled from four unchecked numbers is exactly the "looks good, isn't"

35
00:01:56,571 --> 00:02:01,202
trap 9.5 warned you about, wearing a workshop's shape instead of a quarterly

36
00:02:01,202 --> 00:02:01,751
review's.

37
00:02:02,301 --> 00:02:06,858
This is the shape A12 asks you to fill in, and it's deliberately simple.

38
00:02:06,858 --> 00:02:12,175
One North Star metric at the top, the single number that best represents whether the

39
00:02:12,175 --> 00:02:17,366
feature is succeeding, and the branches underneath it that explain why that number

40
00:02:17,366 --> 00:02:17,999
is moving.

41
00:02:17,999 --> 00:02:22,620
Choose the North Star carefully: it's doing more work than it looks like.

42
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:27,873
For the refund agent this course keeps returning to, deflection is often the honest

43
00:02:27,873 --> 00:02:33,127
choice, the number that ties usage directly to the cost Section seven taught you to

44
00:02:33,127 --> 00:02:33,634
compute.

45
00:02:33,634 --> 00:02:38,761
For a drafting tool, acceptance might sit at the top instead, with adoption and a

46
00:02:38,761 --> 00:02:40,533
cost number as its branches.

47
00:02:40,533 --> 00:02:43,255
Which one is honestly true of your feature?

48
00:02:43,255 --> 00:02:48,256
Notice this tree is deliberately shallow, one level, not a sprawling org chart.

49
00:02:48,256 --> 00:02:51,737
Depth is what the team's own dashboard from 9.5 is for.

50
00:02:51,737 --> 00:02:56,864
It exists to be read in the time it takes to say four sentences out loud, roughly

51
00:02:56,864 --> 00:02:59,776
how long you'll have in the room that matters.

52
00:02:59,776 --> 00:03:04,460
The cost branch is easy to leave off a tree that otherwise feels complete.

53
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:09,777
Pull it directly from Section seven's unit economics model rather than estimating it

54
00:03:09,777 --> 00:03:10,157
fresh.

55
00:03:10,157 --> 00:03:15,031
If 7.2's cost model and 7.5's margin work are already built, this branch is a

56
00:03:15,031 --> 00:03:20,285
five-minute copy, the same shortcut this workshop already gave you for adoption and

57
00:03:20,285 --> 00:03:20,981
acceptance.

58
00:03:21,531 --> 00:03:26,586
Say the honest caveat, because a metric tree is exactly the kind of document that

59
00:03:26,586 --> 00:03:31,702
can look done from a distance while being hollow up close, the same trap 9.5 named

60
00:03:31,702 --> 00:03:34,386
for a dashboard that only ever shows green.

61
00:03:34,386 --> 00:03:39,502
Before you call a branch finished, ask the question a real review would ask: where

62
00:03:39,502 --> 00:03:43,621
does this number come from, and would it survive someone checking.

63
00:03:43,621 --> 00:03:48,613
If the honest answer is "I'm not fully sure," write that down next to the branch

64
00:03:48,613 --> 00:03:53,230
rather than letting a plausible-looking number stand in for a checked one.

65
00:03:53,230 --> 00:03:58,409
This is the same discipline as every honest caveat this course has asked for, aimed

66
00:03:58,409 --> 00:04:03,401
one last time at the artifact you're building right now, not a hypothetical one.

67
00:04:03,401 --> 00:04:08,455
A metric tree presented once and never revisited goes stale exactly the way 8.1's

68
00:04:08,455 --> 00:04:13,635
register does, and the fix is identical: a real owner, and a real date for the next

69
00:04:13,635 --> 00:04:14,383
honest look.

70
00:04:14,933 --> 00:04:19,638
That's today's bar, held to the same standard as every workshop before this one:

71
00:04:19,638 --> 00:04:24,402
attempted honestly, with the one choice that matters most, the North Star itself,

72
00:04:24,402 --> 00:04:26,695
made on purpose rather than by default.

73
00:04:26,695 --> 00:04:30,400
If you finish with time left, don't polish every branch evenly.

74
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:35,340
Return to whichever number you're least confident defending live, because that's the

75
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:37,340
one a real review will find first.

76
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:42,045
Most people building their first tree default to whichever metric was easiest to

77
00:04:42,045 --> 00:04:44,809
pull, not the one that best represents success.

78
00:04:44,809 --> 00:04:49,631
Catch that instinct specifically at the North Star box, because every other branch

79
00:04:49,631 --> 00:04:54,159
inherits its meaning from whether that top choice was actually the right one.

80
00:04:54,709 --> 00:04:56,451
One thing before you move on.

81
00:04:56,451 --> 00:05:01,376
Complete every branch for a feature you actually own, and make sure the top of the

82
00:05:01,376 --> 00:05:06,301
tree was a deliberate choice, not whichever number happened to be easiest to find.

83
00:05:06,301 --> 00:05:11,165
Then hand it to whoever actually presents this feature next, and ask which branch

84
00:05:11,165 --> 00:05:12,727
they'd push back on first.

85
00:05:12,727 --> 00:05:15,249
Neither step requires new data collection.

86
00:05:15,249 --> 00:05:20,054
Everything the tree needs should already exist somewhere in what 9.1 through 9.5

87
00:05:20,054 --> 00:05:21,195
asked you to check.

88
00:05:21,195 --> 00:05:26,000
This workshop is assembly, and assembly is the part most teams skip, not because

89
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,504
it's hard, but because nobody ever put twenty minutes on a calendar for it.

90
00:05:30,504 --> 00:05:35,489
Next lecture closes this section: what these seven lectures actually built, and how

91
00:05:35,489 --> 00:05:39,333
it plugs directly into Section ten, where the job itself starts.
